To add a new method `setSelectedTaskUniqueId` to your task slice, you can simply define it within the `reducers` object of your `createSlice` function. Here's how you can modify your `taskSlice.js` file:

```javascript
import { createAsyncThunk, createSlice } from "@reduxjs/toolkit";
import { BACKEND_APPLICATION_BASE_URL } from "../common/globalConstants";

// Define an initial state for tasks
const initialState = {
  selectedTaskUniqueId: null,
  tasks: [],
  status: "idle",
  error: null,
};

// Create an async thunk to fetch tasks from the API
export const fetchTasks = createAsyncThunk("tasks/fetchTasks", async () => {
  const response = await fetch(`${BACKEND_APPLICATION_BASE_URL}/tasks`);
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error("Failed to fetch tasks");
  }
  const data = await response.json();
  return data;
});

// Create an async thunk to save a task to the API
export const saveTask = createAsyncThunk("tasks/saveTask", async (taskData) => {
  const response = await fetch(`${BACKEND_APPLICATION_BASE_URL}/tasks`, {
    method: "POST",
    headers: {
      "Content-Type": "application/json",
    },
    body: JSON.stringify(taskData),
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error("Failed to save task");
  }

  const data = await response.json();
  return data;
});

// Create an async thunk to update a task in the API
export const updateTask = createAsyncThunk(
  "tasks/updateTask",
  async ({ taskId, taskData }) => {
    const response = await fetch(
      `${BACKEND_APPLICATION_BASE_URL}/tasks/${taskId}`,
      {
        method: "PUT",
        headers: {
          "Content-Type": "application/json",
        },
        body: JSON.stringify(taskData),
      }
    );

    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error("Failed to update task");
    }

    const data = await response.json();
    return data;
  }
);

// Create a task slice
const taskSlice = createSlice({
  name: "tasks",
  initialState,
  reducers: {
    setSelectedTaskUniqueId: (state, action) => {
      state.selectedTaskUniqueId = action.payload;
    },
  },
  extraReducers: (builder) => {
    builder
      .addCase(fetchTasks.pending, (state) => {
        state.status = "loading";
      })
      .addCase(fetchTasks.fulfilled, (state, action) => {
        state.status = "succeeded";
        state.tasks = action.payload;
      })
      .addCase(fetchTasks.rejected, (state, action) => {
        state.status = "failed";
        state.error = action.error.message;
      });
  },
});

// Export the async thunks
export { fetchTasks, saveTask, updateTask };

// Export the reducer and actions
export const { setSelectedTaskUniqueId } = taskSlice.actions;
export default taskSlice.reducer;
```

With this modification, you have added a new reducer `setSelectedTaskUniqueId` to your task slice, which can be dispatched to set the `selectedTaskUniqueId` state. You can dispatch this action from your components to update the selected task unique ID in the Redux store.

To include directories and their children in the file list, you can modify the function to handle directories recursively. Here's the updated version:

```javascript
const createFileList = (dir, fileList = []) => {
    const files = fs.readdirSync(dir);
    files.forEach(file => {
        const filePath = path.join(dir, file);
        const relativePath = path.relative(publicFolderPath, filePath);
        const stats = fs.statSync(filePath);
        if (stats.isDirectory()) {
            // Add directory to file list
            fileList.push({
                fileType: 'directory',
                name: relativePath,
                path: relativePath
            });
            // Recursively process children of the directory
            createFileList(filePath, fileList);
        } else {
            // Add file to file list
            fileList.push({
                fileType: 'file',
                name: relativePath,
                path: relativePath
            });
        }
    });
    return fileList;
};
```

In this updated version:
- When a directory is encountered (`stats.isDirectory()`), it's added to the `fileList` with the `fileType` set to `'directory'`.
- Then, the function is called recursively to process the children of the directory.
- Files are added to the `fileList` as before, with the `fileType` set to `'file'`.

The issue lies in the nested routing structure. The `questions/:qid` route should be nested inside the `:id` route. In the current setup, `questions/:qid` is not correctly recognized as a nested route under `:id`. To fix this, we need to adjust the routing structure so that `questions/:qid` is nested correctly within the `:id` route.

Here's the corrected routing setup:

```jsx
import { BrowserRouter as Router, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';
import InterviewMgmtBase from './InterviewMgmtBase';
import EditCategory from './EditCategory';
import ViewCategoryDetails from './ViewCategoryDetails';
import ViewQuestionDetails from './ViewQuestionDetails';
import CreateCategory from './CreateCategory';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <Router>
      <Routes>
        <Route path="/interview-mgmt" element={<InterviewMgmtBase />}>
          <Route path="create" element={<CreateCategory />} />
          <Route path=":id" element={<ViewCategoryDetails />}>
            <Route path="edit" element={<EditCategory />} />
            <Route path="questions/:qid" element={<ViewQuestionDetails />} />
          </Route>
        </Route>
      </Routes>
    </Router>
  );
};

export default App;
```

### Explanation:
1. **Reordered Routes**: The `create` route is moved to the top to keep the routes organized and avoid potential conflicts.
2. **Nested Routes**: The `edit` and `questions/:qid` routes are nested inside the `:id` route to ensure they are recognized as sub-routes of `:id`.

### Route Structure
- `/interview-mgmt` - `InterviewMgmtBase` component
  - `/interview-mgmt/create` - `CreateCategory` component
  - `/interview-mgmt/:id` - `ViewCategoryDetails` component
    - `/interview-mgmt/:id/edit` - `EditCategory` component
    - `/interview-mgmt/:id/questions/:qid` - `ViewQuestionDetails` component

With this structure, navigating to `http://127.0.0.1:3002/interview-mgmt/6a117152-889b-4aeb-b625-5a8bb2a0c92e/questions/6d09b1ed-12d3-40e3-8f0b-d918feef7b0a` should correctly match the route and render the `ViewQuestionDetails` component as expected.

You can achieve similar functionality in React by using the `useRef` and `useEffect` hooks to scroll the selected element into view when the component mounts or updates. Here's how you can convert the event in your `ListTasks` component:

```javascript
import React, { useEffect, useRef } from "react";
import { useDispatch, useSelector } from "react-redux";
import { fetchTasks, setSelectedTaskUniqueId } from "./path/to/taskSlice";
import { useNavigate } from "react-router-dom";
import CustomButton from "./path/to/CustomButton";

const ListTasks = () => {
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  const navigate = useNavigate();
  const tasks = useSelector((state) => state.tasks.tasks);
  const selectedTaskUniqueId = useSelector((state) => state.tasks.selectedTaskUniqueId);

  const selectedElementRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    dispatch(fetchTasks());
  }, [dispatch]);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (selectedElementRef.current) {
      selectedElementRef.current.scrollIntoView({
        behavior: "smooth",
        block: "nearest",
        inline: "start",
      });
    }
  }, [selectedTaskUniqueId]);

  const handleLinkSelection = (selectedItem) => {
    dispatch(setSelectedTaskUniqueId(selectedItem.uniqueId));
    navigate(`${selectedItem.uniqueId}`);
  };

  return (
    <>
      <div className="linksContainer">
        <div className="left-section">
          <CustomButton onClick={() => dispatch(fetchTasks())}>Refresh</CustomButton>
          {tasks && tasks.length > 0 && (
            <ul>
              {tasks.map((task) => (
                <li key={task.uniqueId}>
                  <span
                    ref={selectedTaskUniqueId === task.uniqueId ? selectedElementRef : null}
                    onClick={() => handleLinkSelection(task)}
                    style={selectedTaskUniqueId === task.uniqueId ? { fontWeight: "bold" } : {}}
                  >
                    {task.title}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          )}
        </div>
        <div className="right-section">
          <div>
            {/* Render your right section content here */}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </>
  );
};

export default ListTasks;
```

In this code:

- We use the `useRef` hook to create a reference (`selectedElementRef`) that will be assigned to the selected element.
- We update the `selectedElementRef` whenever the `selectedTaskUniqueId` changes to point to the selected element.
- We use the `useEffect` hook to scroll the selected element into view whenever `selectedTaskUniqueId` changes.

