To allow `body-parser` to handle larger JSON payloads up to 100MB in your Node.js Express application, you need to adjust the configuration options for `body-parser`. Here’s how you can do it:

1. **Install `body-parser`**:
   If you haven't already installed `body-parser`, install it using npm:

   ```bash
   npm install body-parser
   ```

2. **Update `server.js`**:
   Modify your `server.js` (or `src/server.js` as per your updated `package.json`) to configure `body-parser` with a larger limit:

   ```javascript
   import express from 'express';
   import bodyParser from 'body-parser';
   import cors from 'cors';
   import employeeRoutes from './routes/employeeRoutes.js';

   const app = express();
   const port = 3000;

   // Middleware
   app.use(cors());
   app.use(bodyParser.json({ limit: '100mb' })); // Set body-parser limit to 100MB

   // Routes
   app.use('/api', employeeRoutes);

   // Start the server
   app.listen(port, () => {
     console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${port}`);
   });
   ```

3. **Explanation**:
   - `import bodyParser from 'body-parser';`: Imports the `body-parser` middleware.
   - `app.use(bodyParser.json({ limit: '100mb' }));`: Configures `body-parser` to parse JSON payloads up to 100MB in size. The `{ limit: '100mb' }` option specifies the maximum request body size.

4. **Testing**:
   After making these changes, your Express server will be able to handle larger JSON payloads (up to 100MB) sent in requests. This is particularly useful when dealing with file uploads or large data transfers between client and server.

5. **Note**:
   - Adjust the limit (`'100mb'` in this case) according to your specific application needs. Setting a higher limit increases memory usage, so it should be set based on your server’s capabilities and the typical size of requests your application expects to handle.

By setting the `body-parser` limit to 100MB, you ensure that your Node.js Express application can handle larger JSON payloads effectively, meeting the requirements of applications that deal with significant data transfers.

To organize the Node.js Express project into meaningful files, we'll separate the application logic into different modules for better maintainability. Here's how you can structure the project:

1. **Folder Structure:**
   ```
   - /src
     - /controllers
       - employeeController.js
     - /models
       - Employee.js
     - /routes
       - employeeRoutes.js
     - server.js
   - package.json
   ```

2. **Files Description:**

   - **`server.js`**: This file will be the entry point of our application where we initialize and start the Express server.

   - **`/controllers/employeeController.js`**: This file will contain the business logic for handling CRUD operations on employees.

   - **`/models/Employee.js`**: This file defines the structure (schema) of an Employee model.

   - **`/routes/employeeRoutes.js`**: This file will define the RESTful API routes for employees.

3. **Implementation:**

   **`server.js`**
   ```javascript
   const express = require('express');
   const bodyParser = require('body-parser');
   const employeeRoutes = require('./routes/employeeRoutes');

   const app = express();
   const port = 3000;

   app.use(bodyParser.json());

   // Routes
   app.use('/api', employeeRoutes);

   // Start the server
   app.listen(port, () => {
     console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${port}`);
   });
   ```

   **`/models/Employee.js`**
   ```javascript
   class Employee {
     constructor(id, name, position) {
       this.id = id;
       this.name = name;
       this.position = position;
     }
   }

   module.exports = Employee;
   ```

   **`/controllers/employeeController.js`**
   ```javascript
   const Employee = require('../models/Employee');

   // Dummy employee data stored as constant array
   let employees = [
     new Employee(1, 'John Doe', 'Developer'),
     new Employee(2, 'Jane Smith', 'Designer'),
     new Employee(3, 'Mike Johnson', 'Manager')
   ];

   // Get all employees
   const getAllEmployees = (req, res) => {
     res.json(employees);
   };

   // Get employee by ID
   const getEmployeeById = (req, res) => {
     const id = parseInt(req.params.id);
     const employee = employees.find(emp => emp.id === id);
     if (!employee) {
       res.status(404).send('Employee not found');
     } else {
       res.json(employee);
     }
   };

   // Create a new employee
   const createEmployee = (req, res) => {
     const { name, position } = req.body;
     const id = employees.length + 1;
     const newEmployee = new Employee(id, name, position);
     employees.push(newEmployee);
     res.status(201).json(newEmployee);
   };

   // Update an employee by ID
   const updateEmployeeById = (req, res) => {
     const id = parseInt(req.params.id);
     const { name, position } = req.body;
     const index = employees.findIndex(emp => emp.id === id);
     if (index === -1) {
       res.status(404).send('Employee not found');
     } else {
       employees[index] = new Employee(id, name, position);
       res.json(employees[index]);
     }
   };

   // Delete an employee by ID
   const deleteEmployeeById = (req, res) => {
     const id = parseInt(req.params.id);
     const index = employees.findIndex(emp => emp.id === id);
     if (index === -1) {
       res.status(404).send('Employee not found');
     } else {
       const deletedEmployee = employees.splice(index, 1);
       res.json(deletedEmployee[0]);
     }
   };

   module.exports = {
     getAllEmployees,
     getEmployeeById,
     createEmployee,
     updateEmployeeById,
     deleteEmployeeById
   };
   ```

   **`/routes/employeeRoutes.js`**
   ```javascript
   const express = require('express');
   const router = express.Router();
   const {
     getAllEmployees,
     getEmployeeById,
     createEmployee,
     updateEmployeeById,
     deleteEmployeeById
   } = require('../controllers/employeeController');

   // Define routes
   router.get('/employees', getAllEmployees);
   router.get('/employees/:id', getEmployeeById);
   router.post('/employees', createEmployee);
   router.put('/employees/:id', updateEmployeeById);
   router.delete('/employees/:id', deleteEmployeeById);

   module.exports = router;
   ```

4. **Explanation:**

   - **`server.js`**: Initializes the Express application, sets up middleware (like `body-parser`), and mounts the employee routes defined in `employeeRoutes.js`.

   - **`Employee.js`**: Defines the `Employee` class representing the structure of an employee object.

   - **`employeeController.js`**: Contains functions (`getAllEmployees`, `getEmployeeById`, etc.) that handle the business logic for CRUD operations on employees using the `Employee` model.

   - **`employeeRoutes.js`**: Defines the RESTful API routes using Express Router, delegating the route handling to functions defined in `employeeController.js`.

This structure separates concerns (routing, business logic, model definition) into distinct files, making the application more modular and easier to maintain.

To allow universally (`'*'`) using CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) in your Node.js Express application, you'll need to install the `cors` middleware and configure it in your Express setup. Here's how you can do it:

1. **Install `cors`**:
   First, install the `cors` middleware package:

   ```bash
   npm install cors
   ```

2. **Update `server.js`**:
   Modify your `server.js` (or `src/server.js` as per your updated `package.json`) to use `cors` middleware and allow universally:

   ```javascript
   import express from 'express';
   import bodyParser from 'body-parser';
   import cors from 'cors'; // Import cors package

   import employeeRoutes from './routes/employeeRoutes.js';

   const app = express();
   const port = 3000;

   // Middleware
   app.use(bodyParser.json());
   app.use(cors()); // Use cors middleware to allow all origins

   // Routes
   app.use('/api', employeeRoutes);

   // Start the server
   app.listen(port, () => {
     console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${port}`);
   });
   ```

3. **Explanation**:
   - `import cors from 'cors';`: Imports the `cors` middleware package.
   - `app.use(cors());`: Adds the `cors` middleware to your Express application, allowing all origins (`*`). This means any client can make requests to your server.
   - By default, `cors()` without any options will allow all origins, methods, and headers. If you need more fine-grained control, you can pass options to `cors()`.

4. **Note**:
   - Allowing universally (`'*'`) might not be suitable for all production scenarios due to security reasons. In production, you should consider specifying specific origins that are allowed (`origin: 'https://example.com'`) or using more fine-grained configuration based on your application's requirements.

5. **Testing**:
   After making these changes, your Express server will now accept requests from any origin during development. Make sure your frontend applications can connect to and interact with your backend server without any CORS-related issues.

By integrating `cors` middleware with universal allowance (`'*'`), your Node.js Express application becomes accessible to all origins during development, simplifying integration with frontend applications or APIs hosted on different domains or ports.

