<dependency>
    <groupId>com.squareup.retrofit2</groupId>
    <artifactId>converter-gson</artifactId>
    <version>2.9.0</version>
</dependency>
```

Then, use the following code to consume the API:

```java
import retrofit2.Call;
import retrofit2.Retrofit;
import retrofit2.converter.gson.GsonConverterFactory;
import retrofit2.http.GET;

import java.io.IOException;

public class RetrofitExample {

    public interface ApiService {
        @GET("data")
        Call<String> getData();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        Retrofit retrofit = new Retrofit.Builder()
                .baseUrl("https://api.example.com/")
                .addConverterFactory(GsonConverterFactory.create())
                .build();

        ApiService service = retrofit.create(ApiService.class);
        Call<String> call = service.getData();
        String response = call.execute().body();
        System.out.println(response);
    }
}
```

Each method has its own advantages and use cases. For simple tasks, `HttpURLConnection` or `RestTemplate` may suffice. For more complex scenarios, `OkHttp`, `WebClient`, or `Retrofit` might be more appropriate due to their richer feature sets and better handling of asynchronous operations.

To create a mapper class with generic methods for building success and error responses, you can follow these steps:

1. **Define the Mapper Class:**
   - This class will include two generic methods: `createSuccessResponse` and `createErrorResponse`.
   - Both methods will return a `ResponseEntity<StandardResponse<T>>`.

2. **Implement the Methods:**
   - `createSuccessResponse` will be used to create a successful response with data.
   - `createErrorResponse` will be used to create an error response with a message and status code.

Here’s how you can implement this mapper class:

**`ResponseMapper.java`**

```java
package com.p.java.playground.api.base.mapper;

import com.p.java.playground.api.base.response.StandardResponse;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;

import java.time.LocalDateTime;

/**
 * A utility class to create standardized API responses for success and error scenarios.
 */
public class ResponseMapper {

    /**
     * Creates a success response.
     *
     * @param data        The data to be included in the response.
     * @param statusCode  The HTTP status code.
     * @param <T>         The type of the data being returned.
     * @return A ResponseEntity containing the standardized success response.
     */
    public static <T> ResponseEntity<StandardResponse<T>> createSuccessResponse(T data, int statusCode) {
        StandardResponse<T> response = StandardResponse.<T>builder()
                .status("success")
                .message("Request was successful")
                .data(data)
                .statusCode(statusCode)
                .timeStamp(LocalDateTime.now())
                .build();
        return new ResponseEntity<>(response, HttpStatus.valueOf(statusCode));
    }

    /**
     * Creates an error response.
     *
     * @param message     The error message.
     * @param statusCode  The HTTP status code.
     * @param <T>         The type of the data being returned (usually null for errors).
     * @return A ResponseEntity containing the standardized error response.
     */
    public static <T> ResponseEntity<StandardResponse<T>> createErrorResponse(String message, int statusCode) {
        StandardResponse<T> response = StandardResponse.<T>builder()
                .status("error")
                .message(message)
                .data(null)
                .statusCode(statusCode)
                .timeStamp(LocalDateTime.now())
                .build();
        return new ResponseEntity<>(response, HttpStatus.valueOf(statusCode));
    }
}
```

### Usage Examples

**In a Controller:**

You can use the `ResponseMapper` class to generate responses:

```java
