## Java Exception Handling Programming Questions

### What is the problem with the below program?

```java
package com.journaldev.exceptions;

import java.io.FileNotFoundException;
import java.io.IOException;

public class TestException {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            testExceptions();
        } catch (FileNotFoundException | IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public static void testExceptions() throws IOException, FileNotFoundException {
        // Some code
    }
}
```

The above program won’t compile, and you will get an error message: 
*“The exception FileNotFoundException is already caught by the alternative IOException”*. 

This is because `FileNotFoundException` is a subclass of `IOException`. There are two ways to solve this problem:

1. Use a single catch block for both exceptions:

```java
try {
    testExceptions();
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
} catch (IOException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
```

2. Remove `FileNotFoundException` from the multi-catch block:

```java
try {
    testExceptions();
} catch (IOException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
```

You can choose any of these approaches based on your catch block code.

---

### What is the problem with the below program?

```java
package com.journaldev.exceptions;

import java.io.FileNotFoundException;
import java.io.IOException;
import javax.xml.bind.JAXBException;

public class TestException1 {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            go();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } catch (JAXBException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public static void go() throws IOException, JAXBException, FileNotFoundException {
        // Some code
    }
}
```

The program won’t compile because `FileNotFoundException` is a subclass of `IOException`, making the catch block for `FileNotFoundException` unreachable. You will get an error message: 
*“Unreachable catch block for FileNotFoundException. It is already handled by the catch block for IOException”*.

To fix this issue, adjust the catch block order:

```java
try {
    go();
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
} catch (IOException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
} catch (JAXBException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
```

Note that `JAXBException` is not related to `IOException` or `FileNotFoundException` and can be placed anywhere in the catch block hierarchy.

---

### What is the problem with the below program?

```java
package com.journaldev.exceptions;

import java.io.IOException;
import javax.xml.bind.JAXBException;

public class TestException2 {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            foo();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } catch (JAXBException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public static void foo() throws IOException {
        // Some code
    }
}
```

The program won’t compile because `JAXBException` is a checked exception, and `foo()` method should throw this exception to be caught in the calling method. The error message will be: 
*“Unreachable catch block for JAXBException. This exception is never thrown from the try statement body”*.

To solve this issue, remove the catch block for `JAXBException`.

Note that catching `NullPointerException` is valid because it’s an unchecked exception.

---

### What is the problem with the below program?

```java
package com.journaldev.exceptions;

public class TestException3 {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            bar();
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        foo();
    }

    public static void bar() {
        // Some code
    }

    public static void foo() throws NullPointerException {
        // Some code
    }
}
```

This is a trick question—there is no problem with the code, and it will compile successfully. You can always catch `Exception` or any unchecked exception, even if it’s not in the throws clause of the method.

Similarly, if a method (like `foo`) declares an unchecked exception in the throws clause, it is not mandatory to handle that in the program.

---

### What is the problem with the below program?

```java
package com.journaldev.exceptions;

import java.io.IOException;

public class TestException4 {

    public void start() throws IOException {
        // Some code
    }

    public void foo() throws NullPointerException {
        // Some code
    }
}

class TestException5 extends TestException4 {

    @Override
    public void start() throws Exception {
        // Some code
    }

    @Override
    public void foo() throws RuntimeException {
        // Some code
    }
}
```

The above program won’t compile because the `start()` method signature is not the same in the subclass. To fix this issue, either change the method signature in the subclass to match the superclass exactly or remove the throws clause from the subclass method as shown below:

```java
@Override
public void start() {
    // Some code
}
```

---

### What is the problem with the below program?

```java
package com.journaldev.exceptions;

import java.io.IOException;
import javax.xml.bind.JAXBException;

public class TestException6 {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            foo();
        } catch (IOException | JAXBException e) {
            e = new Exception("");
            e.printStackTrace();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e = new Exception("");
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public static void foo() throws IOException, JAXBException {
        // Some code
    }
}
```

The above program won’t compile because the exception object in a multi-catch block is `final`, and you cannot change its value. The compile-time error will be: 
*“The parameter e of a multi-catch block cannot be assigned”*.

To fix this error, remove the assignment of `e` to a new exception object.

For more information, read [Java 7 multi-catch block](https://www.journaldev.com/629/java-catch-multiple-exceptions-rethrow-exception).
