If you want to avoid hardcoding the increment value in your functionality, you can create a selector factory function that accepts the increment value and returns the selector function. This way, you can dynamically specify the increment value when using the selector in your components. Here's how you can do it:

```javascript
// Helper method to get the next topic ID
export const getNextTopicId = (increment = 1) => (state) => {
  const topicsData = state.topics.data;
  const selectedTopicUniqueId = state.topics.selectedTopicUniqueId;
  if (topicsData.length === 0 || !selectedTopicUniqueId) {
    return null;
  }
  const selectedIndex = topicsData.findIndex(t => t.topicId === selectedTopicUniqueId);
  const nextTopicId = topicsData[((selectedIndex + topicsData.length + increment) % topicsData.length)].topicId;
  return nextTopicId;
};

// Selector factory function
export const makeGetNextTopicIdSelector = () => {
  return (increment) => (state) => getNextTopicId(increment)(state);
};
```

With this setup, you can use `makeGetNextTopicIdSelector` in your components to create a selector that accepts the increment value. Here's how you would use it:

```javascript
import { useSelector } from 'react-redux';
import { makeGetNextTopicIdSelector } from './topicSlice';

const SomeComponent = () => {
  // Create a selector with the increment value
  const getNextTopicIdSelector = makeGetNextTopicIdSelector();

  // Use the created selector with the desired increment value
  const nextTopicId = useSelector(getNextTopicIdSelector(2));

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Next Topic ID: {nextTopicId}</p>
      {/* Use nextTopicId as needed */}
    </div>
  );
};

export default SomeComponent;
```

Now you can dynamically specify the increment value when using the selector in your components, without hardcoding it in the functionality.

