The warning you're seeing is from the `useEffect` hook, which is likely used somewhere in your component. 

When you use `useEffect`, React wants to make sure that you have all the variables and functions that are used inside the effect listed in its dependency array. This is because if any of these values change, React wants to re-run the effect. 

In your case, the warning is telling you that `dispatch`, `id`, and `refetch` are used inside your `useEffect` but are not listed as dependencies. To resolve this warning, you should include these variables in the dependency array of your `useEffect`. 

Here's an example of how you can do that:

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  // Your effect code here
}, [dispatch, id, refetch]);
```

By including `dispatch`, `id`, and `refetch` in the dependency array, React knows that the effect depends on these values, and it will re-run the effect whenever any of them change.

However, keep in mind that adding variables to the dependency array may cause the effect to run more often than necessary. So, make sure you understand the implications of adding each variable as a dependency and whether it's appropriate for your specific use case.

